Janis wrote:
>
> This game of my Caveman seems to evolve very very sloooowly.

Here is my attempt at a skill slot strategy for caveman.  This is my
opinion and very biased.

Max allowed in all schools/classes:

Restricted - All spells but attack and matter, short sword, broadsword,
long sword, two handed sword, scimitar, saber, lance, crossbow, dart,
shuriken, whip, two weapon, riding.

Basic - attack spells (force bolt 1, drain life + magic missile 2,
fireball
+ cone of cold 4, FoD 7), dagger, pickaxe, morning star, unihorn.

Skilled - matter spells (knock 1, wizard lock 2, dig 5*, polymorph 6,
cancellation 7), knife, axe, flail, hammer, polearm (can use knights
move attacks), javelin, trident, bow.

Expert - club, mace, quarterstaff, spear, sling, boomerang.

Master - bare handed combat.


Spell category:
Being able to cast lots of "knock" spells would be nice at the
drawbridge.  But since the caveman's special spell of dig is level 5
don't expect to be able to use it regularly until after the Quest.  To
me
this suggests it is worth spending 1 skill slot on each of the 2
schools
to get to Basic in both.  Do this as soon as spellbooks in those
schools come available, which might well be the middle game.  2
slots, leaves 27.


Missile category:
I am quite ambiguous about enhancing sling past the initial Basic.
Rocks never seem to run out in a throwing game but they weigh
enough that they end up not worth carrying by the time a good stack
of daggers has been accumulated.  And rocks can't be enchanted so
they will never become more damaging than flint or gems plus your
strength bonus.  0 slots, leaves 27.

Early on collect daggers and throw them from your quiver to enhance
to max of Basic.  Even without multifire it is worth 1 slot very early
in
the game.  If given Magicbane as a gift, even better to keep it as the
alternate wielded weapon and switch to it after killing Rodney,  1
slot,
leaves 26.

When centaurs start appearing, bows will become common.  Until
then untrap arrow traps and stash the arrows.  Carry the arrows up out
of the mines with you, down into the main dungeon, stashing them at
the stairs every level until a bow arrives.  Enhance all the way to
Skilled at that point around the time you've finished the mines.  Bless
large stacks of arrows before starting to use them.  After reaching
Skilled in bow keep your bow wielded and use 'x" to switch between it
and your melee weapon.  3 slots, leaves 23.


Non-artifact melee weapon category:
Club is in the initial inventory and so Basic in skill.  It should be
worth
enhancing to Skilled when offered, but switch to the first mace that
appears in the game and never look back at clubs.  Enhancing club to
Expert is something I view as a trap by the DevTeam to get you to
waste limited resources.  2 slots, leaves 21.

Advance pickaxe when offered to basic.  I always seem to forget to
unwield it enough to get offered an enhancement at some point.  1
slot, leaves 20.

The Quest artifact is a mace, so it may be worth getting your pet to
kill a minetown priest after you've contributed all your money for
protection just to get his/her mace.  Also, if you find a mace in a
shop
buy it and switch from your initial club to it.  Until you've spent
your
skill slots on many other items I generally view moving any class to
Expert as a trap, so hold off on Expert until later in the game.  Go to
Skilled as soon as possible and settle for that level until later.  3
slots,
leaving 17.

There are enough spare slots to enhance polearm all the way to
Skilled to make both castles easier.  Once trolls become abundant
and a spetum comes available I consider it worth it to do this in the
mid-game.  Be sure to have your luck maxed out before you start
practicing with your spetum to make sure you hit most of the time.
It is not worth chasing monsters around the dungeon trying to hit
them from range 2 without maximum luck.  3 slots, leaves 14.

Unihorn seems the only other non-artifact weapon worth enhancing to
Basic.  1 slot, leaves 13.

Only advance knife if intending to play a crysknife game.  0 slots,
leaves 13.


Artifact melee weapon category -
A caveman could be either lawfull or neutral so don't bother enhancing
broadsword even if given Dragonsbane (unless you want style points
for enhancing all gifted weapons and are willing to go without some
other enhancements).  Quest artifact The Septre of Might is a mace
that does double damage to most monsters.  Crowning gift is a long
sword.  At the first gifted long sword enhance to Basic.  The crowning
gift is guaranteed to be a long sword and lawfulls will get Excalibur
which is good enough to keep until the Quest. 1 slots, leaves 12.

The Septre of Might is a mace.  After completing the Quest switch to
it and keep it from then on unless you get Greyswandir.  Once you
have it enhancing mace to Expert is no longer a trap to waste limited
resources.  Move from Skilled to Expert.  3 slots, leaves 9.

If given Grayswandir, switch to it and use it from then on.  If given
Werebane, use it long enough to reach Basic and hold it in reserve for
silver hating monsters.  Since saber is restricted, consider not
carrying a silver saber unless given either of those.  1 slot, leaves
8.

If given Mjollnir and/or Cleaver consider enhancing them to Skilled.
6 slots, leaves 2.  The means if you go to Expert in one you are
limited to Basic in the other.  Or use the final 8 slots for style
points
in all artifact gifts, or move club to Expert as well as mace.

Bare handed combat category -
I do not suggest enhancing bare handed combat unless playing for
style.  0 slots.


What order to do the above enhancements?  That's far more
ambiguous than the maximums I have suggested above.  Dagger to
basic as early as possible.  Club and/or mace to Skilled soon
after dagger.  Bow and/or polearm to Skilled after the mines.  The
1-slot prices of spell schools, pickaxe and artifact long sword or
saber as offered.  Maxing out mace about the time of the Quest.

I see a late game caveman with bow as primary wielded weapon
and either Spectre or Greyswandir as alternate weapon for close in
combat.  Quiver contains a large stack of arrows.  The two optional
carried weapons are a stack of daggers to replace the arrows and a
spetum to reach 2 squares away.  A couple of the better artifacts and
some spare missiles in the bag.  All other weapons go in the stashes.
If given Magicbane, pull it from the bag and switch to it once you have
the Amulet to absorb curses.

Cavemen can't two weapon so wearing a shield makes sense for
them.  Without spell casting maybe shirt, GDSM, oilskin, [oR, "oLS,
power gloves, [oESP.  With spell casting maybe shirt, SDSM, robe
(with [oMR in the bag as backup), "oR, small shield, dex gloves,
[oBr, Septre for primary MR.



